{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md", "content": "# Compact-output diagnostic: distinguish tile summaries from noise

In a fresh Continual-ARC instance, the test was a 22×27 partitioned canvas with compact separator-defined regions, but six requested demos had unrelated palettes and dimensions, each yielding compact outputs (examples included 3×4, 3×4, 2×6, 6×2, 5×4, and 1×1). The demos may share the abstract representation of compressing a partitioned canvas, but no exact mapping from each demo's tile contents to output values was derived. Two speculative test submissions were wrong; subsequent requests did not resolve the transformation. This is a diagnostic, not a solved rule.

Reusable decision: prioritize output dimensions and potential partition-to-output mapping over salient test markings. For each demo, count tile rows/columns and map each candidate tile feature (marker color, marker presence, or pattern) to the corresponding output cell; test exact predictions across all pairs. If demonstrations vary, distinguish stable abstract layout from changing tile-level encoding instead of declaring them unrelated at a glance. Never infer test output from output dimensions alone. After a wrong submission, reopen representation and task class, and don't consume further demos without a named ambiguity and different predicted evidence. In any case, the user's interaction contract requires exactly one valid action JSON as the last line on every puzzle response; ensure even a process-limit summary contains a legal action, and do not claim the instance is resolved.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-f0caf098-compact-output-diagnostic.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md", "content": "# Session learning: lattice-like compact outputs

The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/session-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Session note: distinguish invalid grids from incorrect answers

In a Continual-ARC interaction, feedback may distinguish a malformed/invalid ARC grid from a valid grid that simply fails the task. A wrong submission in this session was labeled “not a valid ARC grid”; subsequent candidate construction still failed, underscoring two separate checks: validate serialization and dimensions first, then reassess the inferred task rule based on ordinary incorrect feedback.

Before submitting, verify mechanically that the payload is one JSON action object; grid is nonempty, each row is a list of integer colors 0–9, all rows have the same positive width, and height/width are each 1–30. Check every row. For task inference, compare demonstrated input/output dimensions and full cell deltas before assuming a same-size transformation. A structurally valid candidate is not thereby conceptually correct.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/session-serialization-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/demo-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Validate the demo's output mapping before transfer

A visually structured demo output is not evidence by itself for reflection, tiling, or stamping. After the first plausible but incorrect submission, reopen the task class and output representation before changing coordinates. Record input/output dimensions, map input rows, columns, objects, or symbols to output cells/regions, and compare that candidate mapping against every available pair. Treat exact full-demo reproduction as a prerequisite for submitting; a coherent pattern that fails even one demo is still an unvalidated guess. If requesting another demo, identify which competing mappings its predicted output would distinguish.
